{"uid":"a630d2cf5a972c20","name":"test_an_unrecognised_verdict_value_exhausts_the_ladder_and_still_refuses_the_move","fullName":"tests.test_contract_ladder#test_an_unrecognised_verdict_value_exhausts_the_ladder_and_still_refuses_the_move","historyId":"d6339f349ea604875d3b8837d86e39f7","time":{"start":1791891406940,"stop":1791891406988,"duration":48},"description":"Site 5's own version of tasks 005/006's exhaustion test: a worker\n    payload whose `verdict` the schema rejects outright (never\n    `\"unsatisfiable\"` or `\"complete\"`, so neither of those two branches\n    above this one ever fires) is carried back through\n    `contract_enforcement.enforce_contract`'s SAME re-ask ladder those\n    sites already use, not a single validate-or-abort check.\n\n    The move is refused (the task record never moves off `in-progress` --\n    there was nothing legal to apply in the first place, and nothing was);\n    the ladder ran the FULL `contract_retry_max + 1` attempts (every call,\n    the one charged `run_iteration` plus every uncharged `_ask_contract_fix`\n    re-ask, lands on the SAME `ScriptedRunner.run` (tests/conftest.py), so\n    this call count IS the ladder's own attempt count directly);\n    `sup._abort_reason` stays `None` and no \"contract violation\" log\n    message is emitted; and the run continues per the table (`T(\"worker\",\n    \"no_verdict_yet\")`, the undeclared self-loop -- no `_advance` call at\n    all, exactly like an ordinary `\"no_verdict\"` reply) rather than\n    aborting outright. With the run's own iteration budget already spent\n    by the one charged attempt below (`iterations=2`, one already seeded\n    used), the run ends `timed_out`/`verdict: null` (task 011, v0.24\n    requirement C.1, #169 -- budget genuinely exhausted, the plain shape)\n    via the ordinary tail, never `\"failed\"`/`\"aborted\"`.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Site 5's own version of tasks 005/006's exhaustion test: a worker<br />\npayload whose <code>verdict</code> the schema rejects outright (never<br />\n<code>&quot;unsatisfiable&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;complete&quot;</code>, so neither of those two branches<br />\nabove this one ever fires) is carried back through<br />\n<code>contract_enforcement.enforce_contract</code>'s SAME re-ask ladder those<br />\nsites already use, not a single validate-or-abort check.</p>\n<pre><code>The move is refused (the task record never moves off `in-progress` --\nthere was nothing legal to apply in the first place, and nothing was);\nthe ladder ran the FULL `contract_retry_max + 1` attempts (every call,\nthe one charged `run_iteration` plus every uncharged `_ask_contract_fix`\nre-ask, lands on the SAME `ScriptedRunner.run` (tests/conftest.py), so\nthis call count IS the ladder's own attempt count directly);\n`sup._abort_reason` stays `None` and no &quot;contract violation&quot; log\nmessage is emitted; and the run continues per the table (`T(&quot;worker&quot;,\n&quot;no_verdict_yet&quot;)`, the undeclared self-loop -- no `_advance` call at\nall, exactly like an ordinary `&quot;no_verdict&quot;` reply) rather than\naborting outright. With the run's own iteration budget already spent\nby the one charged attempt below (`iterations=2`, one already seeded\nused), the run ends `timed_out`/`verdict: null` (task 011, v0.24\nrequirement C.1, #169 -- budget genuinely exhausted, the plain shape)\nvia the ordinary tail, never `&quot;failed&quot;`/`&quot;aborted&quot;`.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791891119430,"stop":1791891119430,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791891119431,"stop":1791891119431,"duration":0},"status":"passed"